ach letter selected comes after the one before. To give an example, the word \\(u = business\\) has the word \\(v = bins\\) as a subsequence. In this case, the function used is \\(f(0)=0, f(1)=3, f(2)=4,\\) and \\(f(3)=6\\)\n\nWhile studying protein folding, Lyngsø and Pedersen formed the following conjecture [18]:\n\n**Conjecture 2.1.1.** *Given a circular word* \\([w]\\) *on* Σ_2, *if* \\(|w|_0 = |w|_1\\), *then* \\([w]\\) *contains a subsequence* \\(w'\\) *such that* \\(w'\\) *is an antipalindrome, and* \\(|w'| \\ge \\frac{2|w|}{3}\\).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThree-candidate conjecture for 3-graphs with bounded union — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/341693/three-candidate-conjecture-for-3-graphs-with-bounded-union)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Three-candidate conjecture for 3-graphs with bounded union\n\nLet ${\\mathcal F}\\subset {[n]\\choose3}$F⊂(3[n]​) be a 3-graph.\n\nThree-candidate conjecture for 3-graphs with bounded union\n\nLet ${\\mathcal F}\\subset {[n]\\choose3}$F⊂(3[n]​) be a 3-graph. It has property $U(s,2s+1)$U(s,2 s+1) if every choice of $F_1,\\ldots,F_s\\in{\\mathcal F}$F 1​,…,F s​∈F satisfies\n\n$$|F_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup F_s|\\le2s+1.$$∣F 1​∪⋯∪F s​∣≤2 s+1.\n\nDefine the three candidate families\n\n$${\\mathcal F}_1={\\mathcal A}(1,1,n,3),\\qquad {\\mathcal F}_2={\\mathcal A}(s+1,2,n,3),\\qquad {\\mathcal F}_3={\\mathcal A}(2s+1,3,n,3).$$F 1​=A(1,1,n,3),F 2​=A(s+1,2,n,3),F 3​=A(2 s+1,3,n,3).\n\nThree-candidate conjecture. If ${\\mathcal F}$F has property $U(s,2s+1)$U(s,2 s+1), then\n\n$$|{\\mathcal F}|\\le\\max_{i\\in[3]}|{\\mathcal F}_i|.$$∣F∣≤i∈[3]max​∣F i​∣.\n\nAll three displayed families have property $U(s,2s+1)$U(s,2 s+1); the conjecture concerns the first case for $k=3$k=3 not covered by the preceding general results. The paper notes that this case can have three potential extremal families rather than two, and its status is open.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\nSources & references View supporting material\n\n## Primary source\n\nPeter Frankl and Andrey Kupavskii, “Beyond the Erdős Matching Conjecture”, arXiv:1901.09278 (2020).\n\nextremal-set-theoryhypergraphsunion-intersection\n\n0\n\nPosted by arXiv 6 days ago--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRegular subsequences in finite words (https://wordscomplexity.sciencesconf.org/data/pages/Ryzhikov.pdf)\nciteturn2search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.7 years ago; A highlighted conjecture box with the heading “Conjecture (Lyngsø and Pedersen, 1999)” and the statement: “Every binary circular word of length n divisible by 6 with equal number of zeros and ones has an antipalindromic subsequence of length at least 2/3 n.”\nWeak antipalindromic conjecture\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nA highlighted conjecture box with the heading “Conjecture (Lyngsø and Pedersen, 1999)” and the statement: “Every binary circular word of length n divisible by 6 with equal number of zeros and ones has an antipalindromic subsequence of length at least 2/3 n.” Below it is a circular diagram with alternating 1 and 0 labels placed around the circumference.\n</visual_element>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPalindromic Subsequences in Finite Words (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/331078146_Palindromic_Subsequences_in_Finite_Words)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; In 1999 Lyngsø and Pedersen proposed a conjecture stating that every binary circular word of length n with equal number of zeros and ones has an antipalindromic linear subsequence of length at least .\n\n# Palindromic Subsequences in Finite Words\n\n  * February 2019\n\nDOI:10.1007/978-3-030-13435-8_34\n\n  * In book: Language and Automata Theory and Applications (pp.460-468)\n\nAuthors:\n\nClemens Müllner\n\nClemens Müllner\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nAndrew Ryzhikov\n\nAndrew Ryzhikov\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn 1999 Lyngsø and Pedersen proposed a conjecture stating that every binary circular word of length n with equal number of zeros and ones has an antipalindromic linear subsequence of length at least . No progress over a trivial bound has been achieved since then. We suggest a palindromic counterpart to this conjecture and provide a non-trivial infinite series of circular words which prove the upper bound of for both conjectures at the same time. The construction also works for words over an alphabet of size k and gives rise to a generalization of the conjecture by Lyngsø and Pedersen. Moreover, we discuss some possible strengthenings and weakenings of the named conjectures. We also propose two similar conjectures for linear words and provide some evidences for them.\n\n\n## No full-text available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\nRequest full-text PDF\n\n## Citations (3)\n\n## References (10)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2 WINFRIED HOCHSTÄTTLER AND MICHAEL WILHELMI (https://www.fernuni-hagen.de/MATHEMATIK/DMO/pubs/feu-dmo044-17.pdf)\nciteturn2search15 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 8.0 years ago; **Conjecture 2** (Sticky Matroid Conjecture (SMC) [8]). ... We postpone the proof of Theorem 3 to Section 3.\n*\n\nWe found a proof of this result only for finite matroids (see eg. [7]). We will show that it also holds for infinite matroids of finite rank.\n\n**Conjecture 2** (Sticky Matroid Conjecture (SMC) [8]). *If \\(M\\) is a matroid such that for all pairs \\((N_1,N_2)\\) of extensions of \\(M\\) an amalgam exists, then \\(M\\) is modular.*\n\nThe following preliminary results concerning the (SMC) are known:\n\n**Theorem 2** ([8, 1, 2]). *Let \\(M\\) be a matroid.*\n\n(i) If \\(r(M)\\leq 3\\) then the (SMC) holds for \\(M\\).  \n(ii) If the (SMC) holds for all rank-4 matroids, then it is true in all ranks.  \n(iii) Let \\(l\\) be a line and \\(H\\) a hyperplane in \\(M\\) such that \\(l\\cap H=\\emptyset\\). If \\(M\\) has an extension \\(M'\\) such that \\(r_{M'}(\\operatorname{cl}_{M'}(l)\\cap\\operatorname{cl}_{M'}(H))=1\\), then \\(M\\) is not sticky.  \n(iv) If \\(M\\) has two disjoint hyperplanes then is not sticky.\n\nWe will generalize the last two assertions and prove:\n\n**Theorem 3.** *Let \\(M\\) be a matroid, \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) two flats such that \\(\\delta(X,Y)>0\\). If \\(M\\) has an extension \\(M'\\) such that \\(\\delta_{M'}(\\operatorname{cl}_{M'}(X),\\operatorname{cl}_{M'}(Y))<\\delta(X,Y)\\) then \\(M\\) is not sticky.*\n\nWe postpone the proof of Theorem 3 to Section 3.\n\nWe call a matroid *hypermodular* if each pair of hyperplanes forms a modular pair. With this notion we can rephrase Kantor’s Conjecture.\n\n**Conjecture 3** (Kantor [5], page 192). *Every finite hypermodular matroid embeds into a modular matroid.*\n\nLike the (SMC) Kantor’s Conjecture can be reduced to the rank-4 case (see Corollary 3, Section 5).\n\nNext, we consider the correspondence between single-element extensions of matroids and modular cuts.\n\n**Definition 1.** *A set \\(\\mathcal{M}\\) of flats of a matroid \\(M\\) is called a modular cut of \\(M\\) if the following holds:*\n\n(i) If \\(F\\in\\mathcal{M}\\) and \\(F'\\) is a flat in \\(M\\) with \\(F'\\supseteq F\\), then \\(F'\\in\\mathcal{M}\\).  \n(ii) If \\(F_1,F_2\\in\\mathcal{M}\\) and \\((F_1,F_2)\\) is a modular pair, then \\(F_1\\cap F_2\\in\\mathcal{M}\\).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Sticky Matroid Conjecture (https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.04757)\nciteturn2academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.2 years ago; Title: The Sticky Matroid Conjecture ... Date: Tue Aug 10 15:45:01 2021\nTitle: The Sticky Matroid Conjecture\nAuthors: Jaeho Shin\nDate: Tue Aug 10 15:45:01 2021\n\nWe show Kantor's conjecture (1974) holds in rank 4. This proves both the sticky matroid conjecture of Poljak and Turzik (1982) and the whole Kantor's conjecture, due to an argument of Bachem, Kern, and Bonin, and an equivalence argument of Hochstattler and Wilhelmi, respectively.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAMALGAMATION OF REAL ZERO POLYNOMIALS (https://www.math.uni-konstanz.de/~schweigh/publications/rzamalgam.pdf)\nciteturn2search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.1 years ago; Example 6.1. ... known as the sticky matroid conjecture and was open almost for 40 years until it got ... The proof of Poljak and Turz´ık that every modular matroid is sticky is not very\n≥0 Thus, by Proposition\n2.12 r is stable. But now r(x −1, y, z) is a stable amalgam of p and q by Remark\n2.2(d). This is a contradiction to Example 5.13.\n7. REAL ZERO AMALGAMATION CONJECTURES\nPoljak and Turz´ık [PT1] introduced the following notion for matroids.\nDeﬁnition 7.1. A matroid M is called sticky if whenever it is the common restric-\ntion of two matroids M1 and M2, then M1 and M2 can be amalgamated.\nInspired by this, we make the following deﬁnition.\nDeﬁnition 7.2. We call a real zero polynomial f ∈R[x] sticky if the Real Zero\nAmalgamation Problem 2.13 admits a solution whenever p(x, 0) = q(x, 0) = f.\nIn this section, we will report what is known about sticky matroids and we\nwill speculate about which real zero polynomials might be sticky. We need some\nfurther standard notions for matroids ﬁrst which are based on Deﬁnition 4.4 above\n[Oxl, Pages 28 and 228].\nDeﬁnition 7.3. Let M = (S, I) be a matroid.\n(a) A set F ⊆S is called a ﬂat (or closed) if cl(F) = F.\n(b) M is called modular if r(F ∩G) + r(F ∪G) = r(F) + r(G) for all ﬂats F, G ⊆S\nNote that the condition in (b) says that the submodularity inequality from Propo-\nsition 4.5(c) becomes sharp on ﬂats. Poljak and Turz´ık showed already that every\nmodular matroid is sticky, that every sticky matroid of rank at most 3 is modular\nand conjectured that in fact every sticky matroid is modular [PT1]. This became\nknown as the sticky matroid conjecture and was open almost for 40 years until it got\nrecently solved by Shin [Shi].\nTheorem 7.4 (Shin). The sticky matroids are exactly the modular ones.\nThe proof of Poljak and Turz´ık that every modular matroid is sticky is not very\nhard and it is a very easy exercise for the reader that matroids on two-element sets\nare modular. So we record the following corollary.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRealizable Sticky Matroid Conjecture (https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.14577)\nciteturn2academia18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.9 years ago; Title: Realizable Sticky Matroid Conjecture ... Date: Mon Nov 30 06:53:54 2020\nTitle: Realizable Sticky Matroid Conjecture\nAuthors: Jaeho Shin\nDate: Mon Nov 30 06:53:54 2020\n\nWe give a criterion for modular extension of rank-4 hypermodular matroids, and prove a weakening of Kantor's conjecture for rank-4 realizable matroids. This proves the sticky matroid conjecture and Kantor's conjecture for realizable matroids due to an argument of Bachem, Kern, and Bonin, and due to an equivalence argument of Hochstattler and Wilhelmi, respectively.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKynčl, Pach and Tóth [4, 5] disproved the original conjecture of Erdős (stating that \\(\\ell(N)=\\frac{3}{4}N+o(N)\\)), and showed the following: (https://cs.bme.hu/jh2023/kotet23.pdf)\nciteturn2search19 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.6 years ago; In 1999, Lyngsø and Pedersen [6] studied folding algorithms in the two-dimensional Hydrophobic-Hydrophilic model (2D HP) for protein structure formation. ... One can rephrase Conjecture 1 with this terminology as it states that every binary circular word of length \\(N\\) with an equal number of zeros and ones has an antipalindromic linear subsequence of length at least \\(2N/3 - o(N)\\). ... We are giving a counterexample to Conjecture 2 for this \\(k\\), i.e., an infinite series of necklaces \\(L_i \\in \\mathcal{N}(N_i)\\) where \\(\\mathrm{mono}(L_i)=2k\\) and \\(\\mu(L_i) < 0.6 \\cdot N_i\\).\nOne can rephrase Conjecture 1 with this terminology as it states that every binary circular word of length \\(N\\) with an equal number of zeros and ones has an antipalindromic linear subsequence of length at least \\(2N/3 - o(N)\\). Recently, independently from our work, Müllner and Ryzhikov [9, 10] gave a construction (which is essentially the same as our simple construction) that yields an upper bound of \\(2N/3 + o(N)\\) for both the heterogeneous and the homogeneous models (in this latter model we are looking for a palindromic linear subsequence). It seems that they were the first who studied the homogeneous model, and they made the following conjecture.\n\n**Conjecture 7** ([9, 10]). \\(\\mu^{\\mathrm{hom}} = 2/3\\).\n\nWe disprove all Conjectures above. Furthermore, we improve the best known upper bound significantly by proving the following theorem.\n\n**Theorem 8.** *For Construction 1 (see in Section 2), the size of the maximum proper matching is at most \\((2-\\sqrt{2})n + o(n)\\) in both the heterogeneous and the homogeneous models (i.e., \\(\\mu \\le 2-\\sqrt{2}\\) and \\(\\mu^{\\mathrm{hom}} \\le 2-\\sqrt{2}\\)). Moreover, Construction 1 gives an infinite series of necklaces where \\(\\mathrm{mono}(L)=o(n)\\).*\n\n**Remark 9.** *It is not obvious how this theorem disproves Conjecture 2, so we show the transition. By Theorem 8, there exists a specific necklace \\(L_1 \\in \\mathcal{N}(N_1)\\) with \\(N_1\\) beads where \\(\\mu(L_1) < 0.6 \\cdot N_1\\). Let \\(k = \\mathrm{mono}(L_1)/2\\). We are giving a counterexample to Conjecture 2 for this